{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>These are the generations of the heavens and the earth.</b> It is known that the heavens and the earth are the totality of all created beings that possess body." "<b>And by way of</b> the plain sense (peshat): Scripture writes concerning them the language of 'generation' (תולדה) to testify that they are newly created, brought into being, like this infant that was just now born. And this word cuts off the legs of those who believe in eternity (of the universe)." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: ״אלה תולדות השמים והארץ״ — wherever it says אלה, it invalidates the preceding ones. From here: the Holy One, blessed be He, would create worlds and destroy them until He created these, and said, 'These are pleasing to Me, and those were not pleasing to Me.' The explanation of creation and destruction was in thought, not that they came to actual deed. And I have already mentioned something of this at the verse יהי אור." "<b>And further</b> in the Midrash: Why are 'the generations of the heavens and the earth' and 'the generations' said of Peretz full (מלאים), whereas all other 'generations' in Scripture are deficient (חסרים)? Because when the Holy One, blessed be He, created His world, the angel of death did not exist; and once Adam sinned, the Holy One, blessed be He, installed death in the world, and all 'generations' in Scripture became deficient (חסר). But once Peretz came, his generations were made full (מלא), because from him the Messiah stands, and the Holy One, blessed be He, will swallow up death, as it is said (Isaiah 25:8): ״בלע המות לנצח״." "<b>And by way of</b> reason (sekhel): In the word תולדות, when full with two tav's, they correspond one opposite the other, and their faces are each toward the other — the upper tav is the height of heaven, the lower tav is the depth of the earth. And this is what is written (Proverbs 25:3): ״שמים לרום וארץ לעומק״. The tav of heaven is upright, in order at the head of the word, and the tav of the earth is inverted; therefore, after mentioning 'the heavens and the earth,' Scripture inverted and said 'ארץ ושמים' — to teach you that all of existence is one matter: that above faces downward and that below faces upward, for thus do the generations of both require. And it is possible to say that to this our Rabbis, of blessed memory, intended and alluded in their saying (Exodus 3:14): ״אהיה אשר אהיה״ — 'just as you are present with Me, so I am present with you: if you open your hand and give charity,' as it is said (Deuteronomy 15:8): ״פתוח תפתח את ידך״ — 'I too will open My hand,' as it is said (Deuteronomy 28:12): ״יפתח ה' לך את אוצרו הטוב״.'" "<b>When they were created.</b> Our Rabbis, of blessed memory, interpreted: בה' בראם — 'with the letter ה He created them.' And by way of the plain sense (peshat): without toil and exertion. For there is no letter among all the letters that a person does not need to close his mouth to articulate — except for the letter ה, which a person can produce with the breath of his mouth without any closing of mouth and lips whatsoever, and without any effort. So too the Holy One, blessed be He, created His world without toil and exertion, but with the breath of His mouth, as it says (Psalms 33:6): ״בדבר ה' שמים נעשו וברוח פיו כל צבאם״. And even though the language of making (עשייה) is mentioned in every act of Creation, here it teaches you, when it says נעשה, that that making was nothing but the breath of His mouth. And this is what it means: ״בדבר ה' שמים נעשו״ — and immediately, ״וברוח פיו כל צבאם״." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> בהבראם — באברהם, 'with Abraham'; that in the merit of Abraham the world was created. And so they said: said Rabbi Yudan, בהבראם באברהם — they are the very same letters. And if you are astonished by this, see what is written (Psalms 104:18): ״הרים הגבוהים ליעלים״. Now if the high mountains were created only for the sake of the ibexes, how much more so was the world created in the merit of Abraham. And the meaning of this Midrash is that the world was created in the attribute of kindness, as it says (Psalms 89:3): ״אמרתי עולם חסד יבנה״, and Abraham merited the attribute of kindness, as it says (Micah 7:20): ״חסד לאברהם״." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah:</b> בהבראם — בה' בראם, 'with the ה He created them.' This ה is the final ה in the Name, and it is the smaller one, emanated from the power of Elohim, and it is she who created the world, and she is called 'the hand of HaShem,' concerning which it is stated explicitly (Isaiah 66:2): ״ואת כל אלה ידי עשתה״, and it is written (Job 12:9): ״מי לא ידע בכל אלה כי יד ה' עשתה זאת״. And every instance of Elohim in the act of Creation is teshuvah, and it is the first ה in the Name, and toward it is the intention on Rosh HaShanah, which is the Day of Judgment, on which the world was created, for the Ten Days of Repentance belong to teshuvah. Therefore they enacted in the prayer to say 'מי כמוך אב הרחמים' — with a ה upon the first ה in the Name, and the last ה in the Name, which was added to Abraham, given to him the power of procreation, as our Rabbis, of blessed memory, mentioned: אברם אינו מוליד אברהם מוליד, and as I shall explain on the verse (Genesis 17:5): ״והיה שמך אברהם״. And you should be roused to this: that with the very same sign by which the world was created and founded, that same nature itself altered it — to make known to you that He who brought nature into being is the one who altered it according to His will, for all is in His hand; and He is the one who opens and He is the one who locks, and there is none besides Him." "<b>In the day that YHWH Elohim made.</b> The distinctive Name was not mentioned until now throughout all six days of Creation. And by way of the plain sense (peshat): the name Elohim applies to the act of nature and testifies to the innovation (חידוש), while the distinctive Name testifies to His eternity and existence, blessed be He. Therefore the distinctive Name was not mentioned throughout the entire act of nature — only the name Elohim — because the Torah wished to begin with the account of the innovation; and therefore it needed to mention the Name that indicates the Innovator, which is the epithet that was newly taken up for Him with the creation of His world. And had the Torah's intent been to begin with the account of His eternity and existence, blessed be He, it would have been fitting to mention the distinctive Name that testifies to this. And therefore He revealed what was revealed and concealed what was hidden." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> the Holy One, blessed be He, began to create His world with the attribute of judgment, as it says: ״בראשית ברא אלהים״; He saw that it could not endure, so He partnered with it the attribute of mercy, as it says: ״ביום עשות ה' אלהים ארץ ושמים״." "<b>And by way of</b> reason (sekhel): the parts of existence are twenty-six parts, and these are the ranks of the separate intelligences, and the spheres, and the elements, and the souls — behold, twenty-six, equal to the numerical value of the special Name. And know that these parts were not completed and brought to the perfection of their completion until the sixth day; if so, how is it possible that Scripture mentions the Name HaShem, which is complete, during all the days of Creation in connection with an action that was not yet complete? And you should know that all these parts are included in four, and the four in three, for thus the special Name, which is twenty-six, consists of four letters, and the essence of the four is three, for the fourth is a repetition, and understand this very well." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: He suspended the action upon the special Name, for He is the Agent and He is the Artisan, as mentioned in Bereshit Rabbah: 'The king does not build from his own knowledge but from the knowledge of the Artisan, and the Artisan does nothing except according to what he sees in his intellect and his wisdom.' And this is the secret of the verse in Kohelet (Ecclesiastes 2:12): ״את אשר כבר עשוהו״ — it does not say עשהו but עשוהו, as it were, He and His court."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {3 "Citation (ישעיה כה ח) normalized to Isaiah 25:8.", 4 "Citations (שמות ג:יד), (דברים טו ח), (דברים כח:יב) normalized to Exodus 3:14, Deuteronomy 15:8, Deuteronomy 28:12. In the final citation the text reads ה' for the divine name.", 7 "The kabbalistic passage is rendered hyper-literally with its cryptic allusions intact, per instructions; 'the smaller one' (זעירי) left as written.", 10 "The lemma in the source reads וע״ד השכל — the prefixed ו׳ ('and') is retained as in the source.", 11 "The lemma reads וע״ד הקבלה — the prefixed ו׳ retained as in the source."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:09:48.118208938Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}